From eff1a8827de509bf0b024556c098145d4ff4490b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 17:20:08 +0000
Subject: software_isp: debayer_egl: Filter the cells over time, and time the
 GPU passes

The pass that bins the raw frame into cells blends each cell with the
previous frame's, recursively, with a weight that follows the sensor's
noise as the spatial filter's does, so that what moved is not averaged
and a still scene's noise falls from frame to frame. The cells ping-pong
between two textures; the filter is off for the first frame after the
cells path was last off. The Denoise algorithm sets its strength by gain.

The weight followed each channel's own difference, so a moving edge kept
the old red and blue where the green had changed: coloured ghosts. Take it
from the channel that differs most, which also costs one exponential
instead of three, tighten the motion threshold and the strength limit, and
get the sharpening's detail from the toned linear luma, which does not need
a second pass of the colour processing.

One exponential a tap instead of three, and the channels are no longer
averaged by different amounts at an edge.

With LIBCAMERA_SOFTISP_GPU_TIMING set, and where the GPU has timer
queries, log the average GPU time of the binning and main passes every 60
frames.

A camera switch stops one debayer and starts another in a new GL
context, but the sharpen LUT and the GPU timer queries kept names of the
old one. The first draw then failed with GL_INVALID_OPERATION, the cells
path was disabled for good and the camera stayed black.

A frame that failed to render also never handed its buffers back, so the
next frame tripped the ordering assertion in SoftwareIsp::inputReady().
Return both buffers, in order, on that path too.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
